            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a fair amount of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 



Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around the attic access hatch. 



I recommend you outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket, replace weatherstripping as needed, and using removable caulking to seal around the attic hatch.



I noticed a significant amount of air leakage coming in through your combustion air duct into the basement. This is likely the reason the floor above the combustion air duct is so cold. I recommend that you install an electronic damper on this duct so that cold air is only drawn into the basement when the furnace is firing. 



I was not able to confirm with the infrared camera, but I suspect that there is a significant amount of air leakage at the header in your utility room. I recommend that you air seal at this location and add insulation to the header space.

 

I did note some air leakage through the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $150 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.6 GJ. </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of the basement of your house appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, and your utility room has 1.5 inches of XPS (R7.5) on the walls. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the basement, and replacing the XPS in the utility room with 2x6 framing and R20 fibreglass batt insulation. 



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation to the finished basement walls, and replacing the extruded polystyrene insulation in your utility room, you could potentially save $630 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.3 GJ, and qualify for up to$938 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was found to have 7.0 inches (R28) of fiberglass batt insulation. It was assumed that you have the same amount of insulation throughout the attic, as you have both attic space and scissor trusses over your living room. As you mentioned you may be interested in adding more insulation, I have modelled adding blown cellulose insulation throughout the entire attic for a total insulation value of R50.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R50, you could potentially save $230 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $125 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement floor appears to be concrete slab on grade with no insulation. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the top of the slab. 



If this is an upgrade that you choose to pursue, be aware that you will require drainage under the insulation and that this will reduce the height of your basement.



Savings:

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the floor of your basement, you could potentially save $610 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 178.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $938.50 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program





</foundation>
                <windows>

The windows on the main floor of your house are all newer, triple paned windows that appear to have double low E coatings already. However, the three basement windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass. I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the dining room windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the 3 basement windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $130 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.8 GJ.  </windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $1,080 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 0.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 84.4% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, which also incorporates an ECM fan blower motor. If you choose a high-efficiency propane furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion propane fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE that also incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $1,240 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for water circulation pumps by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick. AEA recommends these pumps for potable water circulation pumps on your city water lines. As this is on a potable water line, you will require a stainless steel pump. When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

On April 1 2019. the AEA launched the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, providing up to $20,000 to help owners of older, less efficient homes achieve significant gains in energy efficiency and home comfort. Completing an exterior wall insulation upgrade is a required first step to participate in this Program, and this must be a minimum increase from the current insulation value by R10. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, there are other rebates available for reducing air leakage, upgrading windows and heating systems, and a bonus for reducing total energy use by a minimum of 20%. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



WOOD HEAT

As you have plans to install a wood fireplace insert in your upstairs fireplace, and remove the pellet stove in your basement, I modelled the potential savings of this upgrade with your current oil furnace. If you proceed with this and use your fireplace to meet 25% of your heating load, you can expect to save approximately $200 per year in heating costs. This assumes you are purchasing wood locally. If you are harvesting your own wood the savings may be greater. AEA recommends a dedicated combustion air supply on all wood stoves and pellet stoves. 



A rebate of 50% of the purchase cost of a wood stove or pellet stove is available, up to a maximum of $1,000. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

